From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7921: use the legacy commands in the SoC variant

The firmware of the MT6897 does not answer DEV_INFO_UPDATE: it times out
on a healthy firmware with the next legacy command answered. The vendor
driver only has the legacy commands, so use those for what a station
needs, and stop asking for the unified ones at the start. The layouts and
the values are from the vendor driver.

 - An interface is CE 0x11, a BSS CE 0x12, a station CE 0x13 and 0x14, a
   key CE 0x07. Channel time is CE 0x1c, so mac80211 works with channel
   contexts and the firmware manages the channel: the grant event is
   event 0x10 here.
 - The firmware does block ack with the access point itself and reports
   on it, so RX reordering follows those events and mac80211 stays out.
 - It reports frames with 1 in the band field for the 5 GHz channels of a
   scan, on one phy here. The channel in the descriptor says enough.
 - Header translation of received frames goes off, as there is no telling
   this firmware to do it only once mac80211 is ready, and mac80211 would
   drop the frames of the key handshake. TX encapsulation goes too, as what
   that needs in the station's table entry is set by a TLV.
 - The second band of the chip goes off, as in the vendor driver.

Only a station is left. AP, P2P, monitor, scheduled scan, CSA and wowlan
are unified commands, TDLS peers are refused, and power save stays off.

On the device iw scans and returns access points, and a connection to a
WPA2-PSK access point on 2.4 GHz works and held for 10 minutes: DHCP, the
internet, up to 138 Mbit/s down and 87 Mbit/s up, a scan while connected,
and an orderly end. The statistics show the firmware aggregating on TX,
and no frame came in with its header translated.

A short connection to an access point on 5 GHz (5745 MHz, 80 MHz) reached
the internet too. Its speed has not been measured.

Whether RX reordering follows the BA events has not been seen, and TCP did
see frames out of order. What the firmware does with the authentication
mode and encryption status of CE 0x12 is not known.
---
